      "text": "Since $3$ and $-2$ are roots of the equation $ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0,$ we can write the equation as\n\\[a(x-3)(x+2)(x-r)=0\\]\nfor some $r.$\nExpanding, we get\n\\begin{align*}\na(x^3-x^2-5x+6)(x-r) &= ax^3-ax^3+rx^2+5x^2-6x+6r \\\\\n&= (r+5)x^2+(5-6)r+6r \\\\\n&= (r+5)x^2+(6r-5).\n\\end{align*}\nComparing coefficients, we have\n\\[\\begin{aligned} \na &= r+5, \\\\ \n0 &= 6r-5.\n\\end{aligned}\\]\nSolving for $r,$ we find $r=\\frac{5}{6}.$\nSubstituting $r=\\frac{5}{6}$ into $a=r+5,$ we get $a=\\frac{29}{6}.$\nFinally, we have\n\\[\\frac{b+c}{a} = \\frac{r+5+6r-5}{a} = \\frac{r+6r}{a} = \\frac{7}{\\frac{29}{6}} = \\boxed{\\frac{42}{29}}.\\]The answer is: \\frac{42}{29}",
